---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Recovery emails">
  <h1>Recovery emails</h1>
  <p>
    An account can keep additional email addresses it has proved it controls.
    Where the project allows it, such an address also signs in to the account,
    so a person who lost their primary mailbox still has a way back in. A
    session opened that way is held for a while from account security changes
    and from handing out access that would outlast it, and every other address
    of the account is told.
  </p>

  <h2 id="turning-it-on">Turning it on</h2>
  <p>
    Recovery emails are off by default and set per project, in the project's
    <code>config_json</code>. The env-configured default project has no
    <code>config_json</code>, so it keeps none.
  </p>
  <Code lang="json" code={`{
  "recovery_emails": {
    "enabled": true,
    "max": 5,
    "reauth_max_age_seconds": 900,
    "sign_in": true,
    "hold_seconds": 604800
  }
}`} />
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Recovery email settings">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>enabled</code></td>
        <td><code>false</code></td>
        <td>Lets a signed-in user add recovery emails.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>max</code></td>
        <td><code>5</code> (<code>0</code> selects it; at most <code>20</code>)</td>
        <td>How many recovery emails one account holds.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>reauth_max_age_seconds</code></td>
        <td><code>900</code> (<code>0</code> selects it; at most <code>3600</code>)</td>
        <td>How recent a sign-in adding or removing an address needs. It cannot be turned off.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>sign_in</code></td>
        <td><code>false</code></td>
        <td>An emailed code or link sent to a recovery email signs in to the account that holds it.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>hold_seconds</code></td>
        <td><code>604800</code>, seven days (<code>0</code> selects it; at most <code>2592000</code>)</td>
        <td>How long after such a sign-in the session it opened is <a href="#the-hold">held</a>. It cannot be turned off.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The two switches are independent. Turning <code>enabled</code> off stops
    new addresses but keeps the ones held: their owners can still list and
    remove them, they still cannot become any account's primary email, and,
    while <code>sign_in</code> is on, they still sign in. Clear
    <code>sign_in</code> to stop recovery sign-ins.
  </p>

  <h2 id="managing">Adding and removing addresses</h2>
  <p>
    All four RPCs need a session. Adding and removing also need a recent
    sign-in: a session whose <code>auth_time</code> is older than
    <code>reauth_max_age_seconds</code> is refused
    <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code> with the stable
    <code>reauthentication_required</code> token, so sign the user in again and
    retry. Anonymous accounts and agents hold no recovery email, and neither
    does an account whose own email is unproven: whoever signed it up may not
    own that mailbox. When the owner proves the address (an emailed code or
    link, or a provider that vouches for it), every recovery email the account
    holds is removed with the sessions it opened, along with the password,
    passkeys and provider links added before the proof.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>RequestRecoveryEmail</code> mails a 6-digit code to the address. Its
    answer is the same whether or not another account holds the address.
  </p>
  <Code lang="bash" code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/RequestRecoveryEmail \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"email":"alice.spare@example.net"}'`} />
  <p>
    <code>VerifyRecoveryEmail</code> confirms the code and stores the address,
    returning the account's addresses. An address is stored only once it is
    proved.
  </p>
  <Code lang="bash" code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/VerifyRecoveryEmail \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"email":"alice.spare@example.net","code":"123456"}'`} />
  <p>
    <code>ListRecoveryEmails</code> returns the addresses, oldest first, each
    with <code>verified_at</code>, <code>last_sign_in_at</code>, its latest
    sign-in, and <code>hold_started_at</code>, when the latest
    <a href="#the-hold">hold</a> it started began (epoch ms, 0 when it never
    signed in), and <code>hold_until</code>, when the account's hold ends (0
    when none is in force). It needs no recent sign-in and works
    with the feature off. <code>RemoveRecoveryEmail</code> removes one address
    and returns what is left; it too works with the feature off.
  </p>
  <Code lang="json" code={`{
  "recoveryEmails": [
    { "email": "alice.spare@example.net", "verifiedAt": "1730000000000",
      "lastSignInAt": "1730700000000", "holdStartedAt": "1730500000000" }
  ],
  "holdUntil": "1731104800000"
}`} />
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Answers when adding and removing addresses">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Answer</th><th scope="col">When</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>, <code>recovery_emails_disabled</code></td><td><code>RequestRecoveryEmail</code> or <code>VerifyRecoveryEmail</code> in a project without <code>enabled</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>, <code>email verification required</code></td><td><code>RequestRecoveryEmail</code> or <code>VerifyRecoveryEmail</code> for an account whose own email is unproven.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>, <code>reauthentication_required</code></td><td>Adding or removing from a session whose sign-in is too old.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>, <code>recovery_hold</code></td><td>Adding or removing from a session the <a href="#the-hold">hold</a> binds.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>, <code>recovery_session</code></td><td>Adding or removing from a session that may be a recovery email's but names none, over <a href="#native-grpc">native gRPC</a>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code></td><td><code>RequestRecoveryEmail</code> for a child account when <code>GATEWAY_MINOR_DATA_MINIMIZATION</code> is on.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>INVALID_ARGUMENT</code></td><td><code>RequestRecoveryEmail</code> for an address a sign-up would refuse: malformed, on a disposable-mailbox domain, or under a reserved or non-routable top-level domain.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>UNAUTHENTICATED</code></td><td>A missing, expired, used up or wrong code, or one sent to another address; or a session a <a href="#removal">removed</a> address opened.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ALREADY_EXISTS</code></td><td>The address is the account's own (<code>RequestRecoveryEmail</code>), or another account's, told only by <code>VerifyRecoveryEmail</code> to a caller whose code was right.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED</code></td><td>The account already holds <code>max</code> addresses, or the caller's IP has spent its budget (below).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>NOT_FOUND</code></td><td><code>RemoveRecoveryEmail</code> for an address the account does not hold.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The code has the passwordless code's lifetime and attempt cap
    (<code>GATEWAY_PASSWORDLESS_CODE_TTL_SECONDS</code>,
    <code>GATEWAY_PASSWORDLESS_CODE_MAX_ATTEMPTS</code>), and every guess,
    right or wrong, counts against the cap. Requests are held to
    <code>GATEWAY_EMAIL_SEND_COOLDOWN_SECONDS</code> per account and per
    address. The per-address cooldown is the recovery codes' own, so a request
    holds back no sign-in code, magic link or password reset to the address.
    <code>RequestRecoveryEmail</code> and
    <code>RemoveRecoveryEmail</code> share one per-IP budget of
    <code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_PASSWORDLESS_PER_IP</code>, and
    <code>VerifyRecoveryEmail</code> has its own of that size. It is the
    emailed sign-in code's variable too, so <code>0</code> turns off all of
    them. These per-IP limits are the HTTP surface's: a host serving native
    gRPC applies its own.
  </p>

  <h3>One address, one account</h3>
  <p>
    Within a project an address is held once: as one account's primary email
    or as one account's recovery email. A primary email cannot be added as a
    recovery email, and a recovery email cannot become any account's primary
    email. A caller who is not signed in cannot tell a recovery email from a
    free or a taken address: <code>PasswordSignup</code> and passkey sign-up
    answer with the decoy a taken primary email gets, and an OAuth sign-in
    with the <code>UNAUTHENTICATED</code> "sign in and link this provider" answer.
    Callers that already hold an account or an admin's authority are told
    plainly with <code>ALREADY_EXISTS</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sign-in">Signing in with a recovery email</h2>
  <p>
    With <code>sign_in</code> on, <code>RequestEmailLoginCode</code> /
    <code>VerifyEmailLoginCode</code> and <code>RequestMagicLink</code> /
    <code>RedeemMagicLink</code> on a recovery email sign in to the account
    that holds it. They never create an account, and they apply the
    account's status, lockout, required second factor and tenant login policy
    as any sign-in does. The project's access policy and the login policy go
    by the account's primary email; an address on a blocked domain, or any
    address in a <code>closed</code> project, is mailed nothing. OAuth and
    passkey sign-in are unchanged: a provider asserting a recovery email does
    not sign in to the holding account. With <code>sign_in</code> off, a code
    or link redeemed for a recovery email is refused exactly as a wrong code
    is, and no account is made on it.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A second factor comes first.</strong> An account with two-step
      verification, or a tenant policy requiring it, gets the usual
      <code>totp_required</code> challenge, and nothing below happens until
      <code>VerifyTotp</code> proves the factor.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The primary email is not proved by it.</strong> The sign-in
      leaves <code>email_verified</code> as it was.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The session says how it was opened.</strong> Its access tokens
      carry <code>amr: ["recovery_email"]</code> and
      <code>recovery_email_id</code>, an opaque reference to the address that
      never names it (see
      <a href="/identity/docs/concepts/auth-model">the access token claims</a>),
      and its refresh token keeps that reference across every rotation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A hold starts</strong>, unless one the same address started is
      still in force, and every other address of the account is mailed.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="the-hold">The recovery hold</h2>
  <p>
    Whoever got into a recovery mailbox must not be able to take the rest of
    the account, take it from the owner, or hand out access the owner cannot
    take back by removing the address, before the owner has read the notice.
    So for <code>hold_seconds</code> after a sign-in by a recovery email, a
    session a recovery email opened is refused
    <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>, with the stable
    <code>recovery_hold</code> token and a <code>RecoveryHoldDetails</code>
    detail carrying <code>hold_until</code>, by these calls, whatever the
    account's role:
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Calls the hold refuses">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">What the call does</th><th scope="col">Calls</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Adds a way in or a way to recover the account</td>
        <td><code>RequestRecoveryEmail</code>, <code>VerifyRecoveryEmail</code>, <code>RequestPhoneVerification</code>, <code>VerifyPhoneCode</code>, <code>BeginTotpSetup</code>, <code>VerifyTotpSetup</code>, <code>RegenerateRecoveryCodes</code>, <code>BeginPasskeyRegistration</code>, <code>CompletePasskeyRegistration</code>, <code>LinkIdentity</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Takes one away</td>
        <td><code>RemoveRecoveryEmail</code>, <code>RemovePhone</code>, <code>DisableTotp</code>, <code>DeletePasskey</code>, <code>UnlinkIdentity</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Replaces one</td>
        <td><code>ChangePassword</code>, <code>RequestEmailChange</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Ends another session</td>
        <td><code>RevokeSession</code> of any session but its own, <code>RevokeAllSessions</code>, <code>SignOutEverywhere</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Deletes the account</td>
        <td><code>DeleteMyAccount</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Connects an agent or an agent machine</td>
        <td><code>GetAgentAuthorization</code>, <code>DecideAgentAuthorization</code> (either decision), <code>RegisterAgentMachine</code>, <code>SelectAgentMachineAgents</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Hires out a teammate</td>
        <td><code>CreateHireGrant</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Registers or changes an OAuth client</td>
        <td><code>CreateOAuthClient</code>, <code>UpdateOAuthClient</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Takes or uses a guardian's control over a child</td>
        <td><code>GrantParentalConsent</code>, <code>CreateManagedChildAccount</code>, <code>GetManagedChildProfile</code>, <code>SetManagedChildPassword</code>, <code>SetManagedChildUsername</code>, <code>RevokeManagedChildSessions</code>, <code>DeactivateManagedChildAccount</code>, <code>ReactivateManagedChildAccount</code>, <code>DeleteManagedChildAccount</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Gives an agent to someone else</td>
        <td><code>TransferAgent</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Changes who belongs to a tenant, or with what role</td>
        <td><code>CreateTenantInvitation</code>, <code>RemoveTenantMember</code>, <code>SuspendTenantMember</code>, <code>RestoreTenantMember</code>, <code>TransferTenantOwnership</code>, <code>CreateDomain</code>, <code>VerifyDomain</code>, <code>SetDomainJoin</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Adds, merges or reactivates an account, or sets a role or another account's password, as a project admin</td>
        <td><code>InviteUser</code>, <code>CreateUser</code>, <code>UpdateUser</code>, <code>ResetUserPassword</code>, <code>MergeUsers</code>, <code>ReactivateUser</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Nothing else is held. That covers the other reads of the account and
    what it holds; calls that take access away rather than hand it out
    (<code>RevokeAgentDelegation</code>, <code>RevokeAgentMachine</code>,
    <code>CancelAgentTransfer</code>, <code>RevokeParentalConsent</code>, a
    project admin's <code>DeactivateUser</code> and <code>DeleteUser</code>);
    the account's own agents (<code>CreateAgent</code>,
    <code>UpdateAgent</code>, <code>AcceptAgentTransfer</code> and the like);
    accepting a tenant invitation or creating a tenant of its own; and the
    other project-admin calls, such as listing users, quotas, groups and help
    requests. <code>RegisterTeammateKey</code> is not a session's call: the
    key manager makes it with a project credential, and it registers a key
    only for a hire grant, which <code>CreateHireGrant</code> makes. In a
    tenant the session acts only through an explicit membership, as the
    limits below describe.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The hold binds that session, not the owner.</strong> A session
      opened any other way (the primary email, a password, a passkey, a linked
      provider) is never held, so the owner can remove an address they do not
      recognise at any time. <code>hold_started_at</code> says which address
      is keeping the account held: the hold runs from the latest one among the
      account's addresses, so removing addresses ends it early only once every
      address whose hold is still in force is gone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The held session can still sign itself out</strong>:
      <code>Logout</code>, or <code>RevokeSession</code> of its own session,
      which it can name only under <code>GATEWAY_REVOCATION_MODE=session</code>,
      where its token carries a <code>sid</code>. One more way it can end the
      other sessions is not held: presenting one of its own rotated refresh
      tokens again sets off replay detection, which signs the whole account
      out, the owner included, as it would for any session. The owner then
      signs in again another way, and the recovery session is gone too.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A sign-in does not extend its own address's hold.</strong> A
      sign-in from an address whose hold is still in force moves its
      <code>last_sign_in_at</code> but not its <code>hold_started_at</code>, so
      the hold's end stays where it was. The hold is measured with the
      <code>hold_seconds</code> configured now, not the one in force at the
      sign-in.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Merges are held for everyone.</strong> <code>MergeAccounts</code>
      and the admin <code>MergeUsers</code> answer the same
      <code>recovery_hold</code> refusal while a hold is in force on either
      account, whoever asks.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Two limits last for the whole recovery session, hold or not:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No hand-off.</strong> <code>ApproveQrLogin</code> from a session
      a recovery email opened answers <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code> with
      the stable <code>recovery_session</code> token (rejecting is still
      allowed): the session the hand-off would open could not say a recovery
      email stands behind it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No membership through the email's domain.</strong> Such a
      session acts in a tenant only through an explicit membership:
      <code>RefreshToken</code> refuses to select, and
      <code>ListMyTenants</code> leaves out, a tenant the account belongs to
      only through its email's domain (see
      <a href="/identity/docs/concepts/multi-tenancy">Multi-Tenancy</a>).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These limits bind the sessions a recovery email opens, not the account.
    Once the hold has ended, such a session makes every call above, from every
    seat the account holds: a project admin's recovery session administers the
    project as their other sessions do. A project whose admins, guardians or
    tenant owners must not be reachable through a mailbox other than their
    primary one leaves <code>sign_in</code> off. Once the hold has ended, such
    a session may also register a passkey or link a provider, and a sign-in
    with either opens an ordinary session, which acts
    in a tenant through the email's domain like any other, as long as the
    account's primary email is verified and on that domain. So disabling a
    member's organisational mailbox does not by itself end their access: a
    personal recovery address can lead back to it a hold later, as a passkey
    enrolled before the mailbox was disabled always could. An organisation
    that wants a departing member out ends the membership itself, with
    <code>SuspendTenantMember</code> or <code>RemoveTenantMember</code>, or
    requires single sign-on in the tenant's login policy
    (<code>sso_required</code>), which no recovery email can satisfy.
  </p>

  <h2 id="after-the-hold">After the hold: back to an ordinary session</h2>
  <p>
    A person who lost their primary mailbox signs in with a recovery email and
    waits out the hold (<code>hold_until</code> says when it ends). Then, from
    that session, they register a passkey
    (<code>BeginPasskeyRegistration</code>) or link an identity provider
    (<code>LinkIdentity</code>), and sign in with it from then on: that session
    is an ordinary one, with no recovery limit. To replace the lost primary
    email itself, <code>RequestEmailChange</code> needs the account's password;
    an account without one keeps its primary email and signs in another way.
  </p>

  <h2 id="removal">Removing an address ends its sessions</h2>
  <p>
    <code>RemoveRecoveryEmail</code> deletes, in the same transaction as the
    address, every refresh token a sign-in by that address wrote, rotated ones
    included, and revokes the session each names. The account's other
    sessions, including those its other recovery emails opened, are untouched,
    and the refused refreshes do not set off replay detection. A session being
    opened by that address while it is removed (a second factor or a date of
    birth being completed) is refused <code>UNAUTHENTICATED</code> instead. The
    ended sessions are audited as <code>session_revoked</code> with
    <code>scope: recovery_email</code> and <code>revoked_count</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    An access token outlives the removal for a while: under
    <code>GATEWAY_REVOCATION_MODE=ttl</code> until it expires
    (<code>GATEWAY_JWT_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code>), and under <code>session</code>
    until the session cache of the replica it reaches drops it (at once on the
    replica that removed the address, within
    <code>GATEWAY_SESSION_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS</code> on others). In either mode
    such a token makes no account security change and hands out no access in
    that time, hold or not: every call the hold refuses, and
    <code>MergeAccounts</code>, answer it <code>UNAUTHENTICATED</code>, since
    its <code>recovery_email_id</code> names an address the account no longer
    holds. An address removed and added again is a new address, so the
    sessions it opened before stay refused. The other calls, such as
    <code>ListRecoveryEmails</code>, still answer until the token expires.
    Removing the address does not take back what its sessions handed out
    after the hold; the <a href="#notices">notice</a> says what to check.
  </p>

  <h2 id="notices">Notices</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A sign-in by a recovery email is mailed to the primary email and to the
      account's other recovery emails. It names the address and when the hold
      ends, lists what the session cannot do until then and says it can do
      everything else, and tells the owner to sign in another way and remove
      the address, which ends the session. A session that outlived the hold
      may already have handed out access that removing the address does not
      take back, so the notice also lists what to check: the account's
      sign-in methods, its connected agents and their delegations
      (<code>ListAgentDelegations</code>, <code>RevokeAgentDelegation</code>),
      its agent machines (<code>ListAgentMachines</code>,
      <code>RevokeAgentMachine</code>) and teammate keys, and the owners and
      roles of its tenants (<code>ListTenantMembers</code>).
    </li>
    <li>
      An added address is announced to the account's other addresses; a
      removal to those and to the removed address.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every notice uses the project's email branding. A notice that cannot be
    sent is logged, and the change stands.
  </p>

  <h2 id="merging">Merging accounts</h2>
  <p>
    <code>MergeAccounts</code> and <code>MergeUsers</code> move the retired
    account's recovery emails to the survivor in the merge's transaction, so
    each still signs in to, and is removed from, a live account. A merge that
    would leave the survivor holding more than <code>max</code> is refused
    <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>: remove some first. See
    <a href="/identity/docs/users/merging-accounts">Merging accounts</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="native-grpc">Native gRPC</h2>
  <p>
    A host that serves identity over native gRPC verifies tokens in its own
    interceptor. The calls above read <code>x-authenticated-amr</code> and
    <code>x-authenticated-recovery-email-id</code>, the token's
    <code>amr</code> and <code>recovery_email_id</code>, only once the host
    sets <code>identityserver.Options.GRPCTrustAMR</code>, meaning its
    interceptor deletes whatever the client sent for either and sets both from
    the verified token. Until then the bridge marks every session
    <code>unvouched</code>, drops the id, and treats the session as one a
    recovery email may have opened that cannot name its address. In a project
    with <code>sign_in</code> on, such a session makes none of the changes
    above and approves no QR sign-in, hold or not
    (<code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>, <code>recovery_session</code>), and
    <code>ListMyTenants</code> lists only its explicit memberships. Elsewhere
    it is refused the same only while a hold is in force on its account.
    <code>RegisterGRPC</code> logs <code>grpc_amr_not_trusted</code> when the
    host trusts the token's other keys but not these. See
    <code>docs/embedding.md</code> in the repository for the interceptor.
  </p>

  <h2 id="audit">Audit events</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>recovery_email_added</code>, <code>recovery_email_removed</code>: an address was proved and stored, or removed.</li>
    <li><code>recovery_email_sign_in</code>: a recovery email signed in to the account, with the method that proved it.</li>
    <li><code>session_revoked</code> with <code>scope: recovery_email</code>: the sessions a removed address opened.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
